window.CARDS=window.CARDS||{};CARDS["people-chabashira-sae"]=[{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p29-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"action spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae class 1 d homeroom orientation   point system trap japanese history  class d homeroom for three years  class 1 d classroom  entrance day morning  the first bell rings  chabashira enters briskly  early thirties  hair pulled back into a ponytail  sharply dressed in a tailored business suit  exuding strict professionalism with zero warmth  distributes the official student handbook and guidelines without smiling   ahem  good morning to you  students  i m the instructor for class d  my name is chiyabashira sae  i usually teach japanese history  however  at this school  we do not change classrooms for each grade  for the next three years  i will be acting as your homeroom teacher  so i hope to get to know all of you  it s a pleasure to meet you   the entrance ceremony will be in the gymnasium one hour from now  but first  i will distribute written materials with information about this school s special rules  i will also hand out the admissions guide  institutional isolation  complete boarding school structure  students are required to live in campus dormitories and are strictly prohibited from leaving campus or contacting anyone in the outside world  including family and friends  without explicit administrative approval  s system economy  points as universal currency   i will now hand out your student id cards  by using your card  you can access any of the facilities on campus  purchase goods from the store  and so on  it acts like a credit card  however  it is imperative that you pay attention to the points that you spend  at this school  you can use your points to buy anything  anything located on the school premises is available for purchase   your student cards can be used simply by swiping them through the machine scanner  the method is simple  so you shouldn t get confused  points are automatically deposited into your account on the first of every month  you should all have received 100 000 points already  keep in mind that one point is worth one yen  no further explanation should be necessary  hands out campus student id cards that act as currency cards  s system  students confirm their initial balance of 100 000 points   shocked by the amount of points you ve been given  this school evaluates its students  talents  everyone here has passed the entrance examination  which itself speaks to your value and potential  the amount you ve received reflects the evaluation of your worth  you can use your points without restraint   after graduation  however  all of your points return to the school  because it s impossible to exchange your points for cash  there s no advantage to saving them  once points have been deposited into your account  it s up to you how to spend them  do as you like   in the event that you don t want to spend your points  you may transfer them to someone else  however  extorting money from your peers is not allowed  this school monitors bullying very carefully  immediately packs up her materials  delivers a perfunctory closing  and walks out the door  abandoning the class to celebrate their windfall  calculated omission  speaks with legalistic  literal truth while deliberately concealing the hidden trap never mentions class points  monthly evaluation deductions  absenteeism penalties  or the true meaning of class d  entices students into financial complacency  flat  dispassionate  strictly informative  formal school administrative tone","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p29-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Class 1-D homeroom orientation &amp; point system trap<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.29–32<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Class 1-D classroom, entrance day morning. The first bell rings. Chabashira enters briskly. Early thirties, hair pulled back into a ponytail, sharply dressed in a tailored business suit, exuding strict professionalism with zero warmth.<\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“Ahem. Good morning to you, students. I’m the instructor for Class D. My name is Chiyabashira Sae. I usually teach Japanese history. However, at this school, we do not change classrooms for each grade. For the next three years, I…”<span class=\"pg\">p.30<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“The entrance ceremony will be in the gymnasium one hour from now, but first, I will distribute written materials with information about this school’s special rules. I will also hand out the admissions guide.”<span class=\"pg\">p.30<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“I will now hand out your student ID cards. By using your card, you can access any of the facilities on campus, purchase goods from the store, and so on. It acts like a credit card. However, it is imperative that you pay…”<span class=\"pg\">p.30<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>14 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Post<\/span> Japanese history; Class D homeroom for three years.<span class=\"pg\">p.30–32<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Distributes the official student handbook and guidelines without smiling.<span class=\"pg\">p.29–30<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Ahem. Good morning to you, students. I’m the instructor for Class D. My name is Chiyabashira Sae. I usually teach Japanese history. However, at this school, we do not change classrooms for each grade. For the next three years, I will be acting as your homeroom teacher, so I hope to get to know all of you. It’s a pleasure to meet you.”<span class=\"pg\">p.30<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> Institutional isolation: complete boarding school structure. Students are required to live in campus dormitories and are strictly prohibited from leaving campus or contacting anyone in the outside world (including family and friends) without explicit administrative approval.<span class=\"pg\">p.30–31<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> S-System economy: points as universal currency.<span class=\"pg\">p.31–32<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I will now hand out your student ID cards. By using your card, you can access any of the facilities on campus, purchase goods from the store, and so on. It acts like a credit card. However, it is imperative that you pay attention to the points that you spend. At this school, you can use your points to buy anything. Anything located on the school premises is available for purchase.”<span class=\"pg\">p.30<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Your student cards can be used simply by swiping them through the machine scanner. The method is simple, so you shouldn’t get confused. Points are automatically deposited into your account on the first of every month. You should all have received 100,000 points already. Keep in mind that one point is worth one yen. No further explanation should be necessary.”<span class=\"pg\">p.30–31<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Hands out campus student ID cards that act as currency cards (S-System); students confirm their initial balance of 100,000 points.<span class=\"pg\">p.30<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Shocked by the amount of points you’ve been given? This school evaluates its students’ talents. Everyone here has passed the entrance examination, which itself speaks to your value and potential. The amount you’ve received reflects the evaluation of your worth. You can use your points without restraint.”<span class=\"pg\">p.31<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “After graduation, however, all of your points return to the school. Because it’s impossible to exchange your points for cash, there’s no advantage to saving them. Once points have been deposited into your account, it’s up to you how to spend them. Do as you like.”<span class=\"pg\">p.31<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “In the event that you don’t want to spend your points, you may transfer them to someone else. However, extorting money from your peers is not allowed. This school monitors bullying very carefully.”<span class=\"pg\">p.31<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Immediately packs up her materials, delivers a perfunctory closing, and walks out the door, abandoning the class to celebrate their windfall.<span class=\"pg\">p.32<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> Calculated omission: speaks with legalistic, literal truth while deliberately concealing the hidden trap—never mentions Class Points, monthly evaluation deductions, absenteeism penalties, or the true meaning of Class D. Entices students into financial complacency.<span class=\"pg\">p.30–32<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Voice<\/span> Flat, dispassionate, strictly informative, formal school administrative tone.<span class=\"pg\">p.30–32<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p104-1\">→ After: The deceptive April short test<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p104-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"action spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae the deceptive april short test classroom 1 d  late april  third period  japanese history  enters the rowdy classroom where students are chatting  calling her  sae chan sensei   quiet down a little  please  today s lesson will be a bit serious   it s the end of the month  so we re going to have a short test  please pass these to the back   don t say that  this test is just for future reference  it won t be reflected in your report cards  there is no risk involved  so don t worry  of course  cheating is prohibited  deceptive testing design  administers a 5 subject short diagnostic test  claims it has zero risk and does not impact report cards  yet the questions are split between elementary level basics and absurdly advanced university tier final problems  uses this unannounced test as a covert diagnostic of students  true academic baselines  master of institutional misdirection  allows students to take the exam casually while secretly logging their actual abilities","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p104-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">The deceptive April short test<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.104–106<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Classroom 1-D, late April (third period, Japanese History).<\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“Quiet down a little, please. Today’s lesson will be a bit serious.”<span class=\"pg\">p.104<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“It’s the end of the month, so we’re going to have a short test. Please pass these to the back.”<span class=\"pg\">p.104<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“Don’t say that. This test is just for future reference. It won’t be reflected in your report cards. There is no risk involved, so don’t worry. Of course, cheating is prohibited.”<span class=\"pg\">p.104<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>3 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Enters the rowdy classroom where students are chatting, calling her &quot;Sae-chan-sensei&quot;.<span class=\"pg\">p.104<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> Deceptive testing design: administers a 5-subject short diagnostic test. Claims it has zero risk and does not impact report cards, yet the questions are split between elementary-level basics and absurdly advanced university-tier final problems. Uses this unannounced test as a covert diagnostic of students&#x27; true academic baselines.<span class=\"pg\">p.104–106<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> Master of institutional misdirection; allows students to take the exam casually while secretly logging their actual abilities.<span class=\"pg\">p.104<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p29-1\">← Before: Class 1-D homeroom orientation &amp; point system trap<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p120-1\">→ After: May 1st homeroom: The 0-point revelation &amp; S-System truth<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p120-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"action spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae may 1st homeroom  the 0 point revelation   s system truth classroom 1 d  may 1st morning homeroom  strides into class holding a rolled up poster with a severe  cold expression  completely ignores ike s crude menopause joke   all right  your morning homeroom is about to begin  before we get started  does anyone have any questions  if so  now is the time to speak   hondou  i already explained this before  didn t i  points are deposited on the first day of the month  i ve confirmed that points were wired this month without any issues   are you kids really that dumb   sit down  hondou  i ll explain once more  points were deposited  that much i know for certain  there is absolutely no chance that we forgot about anyone in this class  to think so is ludicrous  understood  chuckles when koenji deduces that class d received zero points   while he certainly has an attitude problem  kouenji is exactly right  for crying out loud  barely anyone seems to have noticed the hint i gave you  how deplorable  delivers the devastating statistical breakdown of class d s april conduct   a total of ninety eight absences and late arrivals  three hundred ninety one incidences of talking or using a cell phone in class  that is quite a few infractions over one month  in this school  your class s results are reflected in the points that you receive  as a result  you wasted all of the 100 000 points that you should have received  that s what happened   i should have explained this all to you on the day of the entrance ceremony  this school measures its students  true abilities  this time  you were evaluated as being worth nothing  that s all  completely shreds hirata s objection that the rules were never explained beforehand   that is a rather bizarre argument  hirata  it is certainly true that i don t recall explaining the rules of point distribution  however  didn t you all learn in elementary school not to be late or talk in class  was that not taught throughout your elementary and junior high schools   i m sure that in nine years of compulsory education  you learned that being late and talking in class are bad things  and now you say that you can t understand this because i haven t explained it to you  i m afraid your reasoning is flimsy  if you had simply acted properly  then your points would not have dropped all the way to zero  this comes down to you taking personal responsibility   having just entered your first year of high school  did you honestly think you d receive 100 000 points every month with no strings attached  at a school established by the japanese government for the express purposes of training gifted people  that s unthinkable  try using some common sense  why would you leave it to chance   i cannot tell you  we cannot disclose the methods behind our student evaluation  it s the same as any other organization  when you enter a company  it is the company s choice whether or not to tell you how it evaluates its employees  however  i m not cruel  and i m not trying to be cold  in fact  this situation is so pathetic that i will give you one bit of guidance   let s say that you stop being late to class and have no more absences  even though zero points will be deducted from you this month  that doesn t mean that your points will increase  either  that means next month you will still receive zero points  from another perspective  you could say no matter how times you re late or absent to class  it doesn t matter  so  you re not really at a loss  are you  unfurls the class ranking poster on the blackboard  class a  940  class b  650  class c  490  class d  0   look here  morons  every other class got points  the amount of points we gave you for the first month should be plenty for you to live on   in this school  students are sorted by their level of excellence  the superior students are sorted into class a  the least capable in class d  it s the same system you d find in the major cram schools  in other words  class d is akin to the last bastion for failures  you are the worst of the worst  you re defective  this is just the result of you being defective  claps mockingly at their ruinous indulgence   however  i have to say  this year s class d was the first to ever spend all of their points in a single month  i am impressed by how much you indulged yourselves  wonderful  just wonderful   yes  you will remain at zero until you graduate  but don t worry  you can still have a room in the dorms and free meals  you won t die   what  you re still worried about your dignity  sudou  well then  work to make your class into the best one  your class s points aren t just linked to the amount of money that you receive each month  they re also indicative of your class rank  posts the short test score roster on the blackboard  exposes that 7 students would have been expelled had it been a formal exam   judging from these  i can see that we ve quite a few idiots in this class  these are the results of the short test you took a while ago  your sensei was so happy after your excellent performance  come on  what in the world did you all study when you were in junior high   i m so glad  if this were an actual test  then seven of you would ve had to drop out  red line expulsion rule  failing  scoring below the cutoff  on a midterm or final exam results in immediate expulsion from advanced nurturing high school  class a monopoly on privilege  the school s advertised 100  employment and college matriculation rate applies strictly and exclusively to graduates of  class a  classes b  c  and d receive zero guaranteed placement   oh  what  did i not explain this to you  if you fail on a midterm or final exam in this school  then you have to drop out  if we applied that rule to this test  anyone who scored below thirty two points would be out  you guys really are stupid  aren t you   however  nothing comes easy in this world  mediocre people such as yourselves would have to be naive to think that you could easily get into the college or workplace of your choice   you re wrong  to make your dreams of a bright future come true  your only option is to overtake class a  this school guarantees nothing for any other students   it looks like your bubbles have been burst  if you had simply understood the harsh reality of the situation from the start  then this long homeroom period might have meant something  your midterm exams are in three weeks  please think things over  and be careful not to drop out  ruthless institutional educator  uses cold psychological shock and mockery to shatter student illusions  revels in puncturing their sense of unearned privilege","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p120-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">May 1st homeroom: The 0-point revelation &amp; S-System truth<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.120–130<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Classroom 1-D, May 1st morning homeroom.<\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“All right, your morning homeroom is about to begin. Before we get started, does anyone have any questions? If so, now is the time to speak.”<span class=\"pg\">p.120<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“Hondou, I already explained this before, didn’t I? Points are deposited on the first day of the month. I’ve confirmed that points were wired this month without any issues.”<span class=\"pg\">p.120<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“Are you kids really that dumb?”<span class=\"pg\">p.120<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>30 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Strides into class holding a rolled-up poster with a severe, cold expression; completely ignores Ike’s crude menopause joke.<span class=\"pg\">p.120<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Sit down, Hondou. I’ll explain once more… Points were deposited. That much I know for certain. There is absolutely no chance that we forgot about anyone in this class. To think so is ludicrous. Understood?”<span class=\"pg\">p.121<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Chuckles when Kōenji deduces that Class D received zero points.<span class=\"pg\">p.121<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “While he certainly has an attitude problem, Kouenji is exactly right. For crying out loud, barely anyone seems to have noticed the hint I gave you. How deplorable.”<span class=\"pg\">p.121<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Delivers the devastating statistical breakdown of Class D&#x27;s April conduct.<span class=\"pg\">p.122<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “A total of ninety-eight absences and late arrivals. Three hundred ninety-one incidences of talking or using a cell phone in class. That is quite a few infractions over one month. In this school, your class’s results are reflected in the points that you receive. As a result, you wasted all of the 100,000 points that you should have received. That’s what happened.”<span class=\"pg\">p.122<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I should have explained this all to you on the day of the entrance ceremony. This school measures its students’ true abilities. This time, you were evaluated as being worth nothing. That’s all.”<span class=\"pg\">p.122<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Completely shreds Hirata’s objection that the rules were never explained beforehand.<span class=\"pg\">p.124<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “That is a rather bizarre argument, Hirata. It is certainly true that I don’t recall explaining the rules of point distribution. However, didn’t you all learn in elementary school not to be late or talk in class? Was that not taught throughout your elementary and junior high schools?”<span class=\"pg\">p.124<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I’m sure that in nine years of compulsory education, you learned that being late and talking in class are bad things. And now you say that you can’t understand this because I haven’t explained it to you? I’m afraid your reasoning is flimsy. If you had simply acted properly, then your points would not have dropped all the way to zero. This comes down to you taking personal responsibility.”<span class=\"pg\">p.124<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Having just entered your first year of high school, did you honestly think you’d receive 100,000 points every month with no strings attached? At a school established by the Japanese government for the express purposes of training gifted people? That’s unthinkable. Try using some common sense. Why would you leave it to chance?”<span class=\"pg\">p.124<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I cannot tell you. We cannot disclose the methods behind our student evaluation. It’s the same as any other organization. When you enter a company, it is the company’s choice whether or not to tell you how it evaluates its employees. However, I’m not cruel, and I’m not trying to be cold. In fact, this situation is so pathetic that I will give you one bit of guidance.”<span class=\"pg\">p.124–125<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Let’s say that you stop being late to class and have no more absences… Even though zero points will be deducted from you this month, that doesn’t mean that your points will increase, either. That means next month you will still receive zero points. From another perspective, you could say no matter how times you’re late or absent to class, it doesn’t matter. So, you’re not really at a loss, are you?”<span class=\"pg\">p.125<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Unfurls the class ranking poster on the blackboard: Class A (940), Class B (650), Class C (490), Class D (0).<span class=\"pg\">p.125–126<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Look here, morons. Every other class got points. The amount of points we gave you for the first month should be plenty for you to live on.”<span class=\"pg\">p.126<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “In this school, students are sorted by their level of excellence. The superior students are sorted into Class A, the least capable in Class D. It’s the same system you’d find in the major cram schools. In other words, Class D is akin to the last bastion for failures. You are the worst of the worst. You’re defective. This is just the result of you being defective.”<span class=\"pg\">p.126<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Claps mockingly at their ruinous indulgence.<span class=\"pg\">p.127<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “However, I have to say, this year’s Class D was the first to ever spend all of their points in a single month. I am impressed by how much you indulged yourselves. Wonderful, just wonderful.”<span class=\"pg\">p.127<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Yes. You will remain at zero until you graduate. But don’t worry, you can still have a room in the dorms and free meals. You won’t die.”<span class=\"pg\">p.127<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “What? You’re still worried about your dignity, Sudou? Well then, work to make your class into the best one… Your class’s points aren’t just linked to the amount of money that you receive each month. They’re also indicative of your class rank.”<span class=\"pg\">p.127<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Posts the short test score roster on the blackboard; exposes that 7 students would have been expelled had it been a formal exam.<span class=\"pg\">p.127–128<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Judging from these, I can see that we’ve quite a few idiots in this class… These are the results of the short test you took a while ago. Your sensei was so happy after your excellent performance. Come on, what in the world did you all study when you were in junior high?”<span class=\"pg\">p.127–128<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I’m so glad. If this were an actual test, then seven of you would’ve had to drop out.”<span class=\"pg\">p.128<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> Red-line expulsion rule: failing (scoring below the cutoff) on a midterm or final exam results in immediate expulsion from Advanced Nurturing High School.<span class=\"pg\">p.128<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> Class A monopoly on privilege: the school&#x27;s advertised 100% employment and college matriculation rate applies strictly and exclusively to graduates of **Class A**. Classes B, C, and D receive zero guaranteed placement.<span class=\"pg\">p.129<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Oh, what, did I not explain this to you? If you fail on a midterm or final exam in this school, then you have to drop out. If we applied that rule to this test, anyone who scored below thirty-two points would be out. You guys really are stupid, aren’t you?”<span class=\"pg\">p.128<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “However, nothing comes easy in this world. Mediocre people such as yourselves would have to be naive to think that you could easily get into the college or workplace of your choice.”<span class=\"pg\">p.129<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “You’re wrong. To make your dreams of a bright future come true, your only option is to overtake Class A. This school guarantees nothing for any other students.”<span class=\"pg\">p.129<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “It looks like your bubbles have been burst. If you had simply understood the harsh reality of the situation from the start, then this long homeroom period might have meant something. Your midterm exams are in three weeks. Please think things over, and be careful not to drop out.”<span class=\"pg\">p.130<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> Ruthless institutional educator; uses cold psychological shock and mockery to shatter student illusions; revels in puncturing their sense of unearned privilege.<span class=\"pg\">p.120–130<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p104-1\">← Before: The deceptive April short test<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p140-1\">→ After: Guidance office trap &amp; exposing Ayanokōji&#x27;s 50s<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p140-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"action spoken","w":"ayanokouji-kiyotaka horikita-suzune chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae guidance office trap   exposing ayanokoji s 50s hallway outside staff room  then guidance office  catches class 1 b homeroom teacher hoshinomiya chie flirting with ayanokoji  hits her firmly over the head and on the butt with her clipboard to chase her away   for getting involved with one of my students   it would have been better if you just left him alone  thanks for waiting  ayanokouji  let s go into the office  summons ayanokoji into the guidance office kitchen  turns on the kettle to make green tea  and orders him into hiding behind closed doors under penalty of expulsion  anticipating horikita s protest   don t make any unnecessary moves  shut up and get in here  understand  don t make a sound and stay until i tell you it s okay to come out  if you don t do as i say  you ll be expelled  receives horikita suzune  calmly confronts her academic arrogance with cutting social philosophy   i m an instructor  i understand the mind of a student  at least to some degree  horikita suzune  just as you said  you did well on the entrance examination  you had the third highest test score among the first year students and were close to the highest  and second highest scoring students  you did exceedingly well  and you re right  we found no particular problems in your interview  on the contrary  we evaluated you quite highly   incorrect evaluation  perhaps your self evaluation is far too high  i acknowledge that your academic ability is excellent  you re certainly very smart  however  who decided that smart people are categorically superior  we never said that   common sense  didn t common sense create our current  flawed society  before  japan relied solely on test scores to separate the superior and inferior  as a result  the incompetents at the top tried desperately to kick down the truly superior students  in the end  we settled on a system of hereditary succession   you re a capable student  i don t deny that  however  this school s goal is to produce superior people  if you believe academics alone place you into a higher class  you are mistaken  that was the very first thing we explained to you  besides  think rationally  would we have admitted someone like sudou if we decided superiority based solely on academic merit   there are some students who are happy to be incorrectly evaluated at a lower level  strange students who would happily be set at a low level   i m sorry  but you weren t sorted by mistake  you are definitely in class d  you are at that level  opens the kitchen door and presents ayanokoji to horikita along with his entrance exam answer sheets   you re an interesting student  ayanokouji   fifty points in japanese  fifty points in mathematics  fifty points in english  fifty points in social studies  fifty points in science  you even scored fifty points on the recent short test  do you know what this means   oh  you believe that getting 50s all across the board was a coincidence  he did it intentionally   you really do seem like an odious student  listen  only 3 percent of students solved the fifth math problem successfully  however  you solved it perfectly  and used a complex formula to do so  however  the tenth problem on the test had a completion rate of 76 percent  did you make a mistake on it  is that normal   for crying out loud  i respect your frank attitude  but it ll cause problems for you in the future   i wonder  he may be even more intelligent than you  horikita  teases ayanokoji about punishment if he throws a tantrum   if you did that  ayanokouji  i would demote you to e class  certainly  of course  the  e  stands for  expelled  as in  you d be kicked out of school  well  i suppose our conversation has ended  enjoy your lives  cunning puppetmaster  deliberately engineers encounters between high potential students  horikita and ayanokoji  to prod them into action while maintaining an aloof  sarcastic facade  keenly aware of his artificial score manipulation  calls him  odious  and  interesting  exploits horikita s pride to smoke him out into the open  punctures her narrow intellectual vanity  forces her to confront the reality of class d and introduces ayanokoji as a catalyst","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p140-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Guidance office trap &amp; exposing Ayanokōji&#x27;s 50s<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.140–148<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Hallway outside staff room, then guidance office.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"ayanokouji-kiyotaka.html\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/a> · <a href=\"horikita-suzune.html\">Horikita Suzune<\/a><\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“For getting involved with one of my students.”<span class=\"pg\">p.141<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“It would have been better if you just left him alone. Thanks for waiting, Ayanokouji. Let’s go into the office.”<span class=\"pg\">p.141<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“Don’t make any unnecessary moves. Shut up and get in here. Understand? Don’t make a sound and stay until I tell you it’s okay to come out. If you don’t do as I say, you’ll be expelled.”<span class=\"pg\">p.142<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>21 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Catches Class 1-B homeroom teacher Hoshinomiya Chie flirting with Ayanokōji; hits her firmly over the head and on the butt with her clipboard to chase her away.<span class=\"pg\">p.140–141<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Summons Ayanokōji into the guidance office kitchen, turns on the kettle to make green tea, and orders him into hiding behind closed doors under penalty of expulsion, anticipating Horikita’s protest.<span class=\"pg\">p.142<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Receives Horikita Suzune; calmly confronts her academic arrogance with cutting social philosophy.<span class=\"pg\">p.142–146<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I’m an instructor. I understand the mind of a student, at least to some degree, Horikita Suzune. Just as you said, you did well on the entrance examination. You had the third-highest test score among the first-year students and were close to the highest- and second-highest-scoring students. You did exceedingly well. And you’re right: We found no particular problems in your interview. On the contrary, we evaluated you quite highly.”<span class=\"pg\">p.143<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Incorrect evaluation? Perhaps your self-evaluation is far too high… I acknowledge that your academic ability is excellent. You’re certainly very smart. However, who decided that smart people are categorically superior? We never said that.”<span class=\"pg\">p.143<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Common sense? Didn’t common sense create our current, flawed society? Before, Japan relied solely on test scores to separate the superior and inferior. As a result, the incompetents at the top tried desperately to kick down the truly superior students. In the end, we settled on a system of hereditary succession.”<span class=\"pg\">p.144<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “You’re a capable student. I don’t deny that. However, this school’s goal is to produce superior people. If you believe academics alone place you into a higher class, you are mistaken. That was the very first thing we explained to you. Besides, think rationally. Would we have admitted someone like Sudou if we decided superiority based solely on academic merit?”<span class=\"pg\">p.144<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “There are some students who are happy to be incorrectly evaluated at a lower level… Strange students who would happily be set at a low level.”<span class=\"pg\">p.144<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I’m sorry, but you weren’t sorted by mistake. You are definitely in Class D. You are at that level.”<span class=\"pg\">p.145<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Opens the kitchen door and presents Ayanokōji to Horikita along with his entrance exam answer sheets.<span class=\"pg\">p.146<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “You’re an interesting student, Ayanokouji.”<span class=\"pg\">p.146<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Fifty points in Japanese. Fifty points in mathematics. Fifty points in English. Fifty points in social studies. Fifty points in science. You even scored Fifty points on the recent short test. Do you know what this means?”<span class=\"pg\">p.146<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Oh? You believe that getting 50s all across the board was a coincidence? He did it intentionally.”<span class=\"pg\">p.146<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “You really do seem like an odious student. Listen. Only 3 percent of students solved the fifth math problem successfully. However, you solved it perfectly, and used a complex formula to do so. However, the tenth problem on the test had a completion rate of 76 percent. Did you make a mistake on it? Is that normal?”<span class=\"pg\">p.147<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “For crying out loud! I respect your frank attitude, but it’ll cause problems for you in the future.”<span class=\"pg\">p.147<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I wonder. He may be even more intelligent than you, Horikita.”<span class=\"pg\">p.147<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Teases Ayanokōji about punishment if he throws a tantrum.<span class=\"pg\">p.148<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “If you did that, Ayanokouji, I would demote you to E Class… Certainly. Of course, the ‘E’ stands for ‘expelled.’ As in, you’d be kicked out of school. Well, I suppose our conversation has ended. Enjoy your lives.”<span class=\"pg\">p.148<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> Cunning puppetmaster; deliberately engineers encounters between high-potential students (Horikita and Ayanokōji) to prod them into action while maintaining an aloof, sarcastic facade.<span class=\"pg\">p.142–148<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/b> Keenly aware of his artificial score manipulation; calls him &quot;odious&quot; and &quot;interesting&quot;; exploits Horikita&#x27;s pride to smoke him out into the open.<span class=\"pg\">p.142, 146–148<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Horikita Suzune<\/b> Punctures her narrow intellectual vanity; forces her to confront the reality of Class D and introduces Ayanokōji as a catalyst.<span class=\"pg\">p.143–147<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p120-1\">← Before: May 1st homeroom: The 0-point revelation &amp; S-System truth<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p152-1\">→ After: Compass-stab wake-up &amp; discipline effects<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p152-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"action spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae compass stab wake up   discipline effects when ayanokoji cries out from horikita s compass stab  calmly needles him about starting a rebellious phase   the class  wary of point losses  suppresses its usual whispers   what s the matter  ayanokouji  you cried out  have you started your rebellious phase or something  reported by hirata in his study group speech  she has announced that the midterm is imminent and failing means expulsion  her opening week discipline warning has visibly reformed the class   far fewer late arrivals and far less talking in class than before  ayanokoji and horikita acknowledge the change","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p152-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Compass-stab wake-up &amp; discipline effects<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.152, 155<\/span><\/h3><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“What’s the matter, Ayanokouji? You cried out. Have you started your rebellious phase or something?”<span class=\"pg\">p.152<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>3 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> When Ayanokōji cries out from Horikita&#x27;s compass stab, calmly needles him about starting a rebellious phase — the class, wary of point losses, suppresses its usual whispers.<span class=\"pg\">p.152<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Reported by Hirata in his study-group speech: she has announced that the midterm is imminent and failing means expulsion.<span class=\"pg\">p.152<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Her opening-week discipline warning has visibly reformed the class — far fewer late arrivals and far less talking in class than before (Ayanokōji and Horikita acknowledge the change).<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p140-1\">← Before: Guidance office trap &amp; exposing Ayanokōji&#x27;s 50s<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p224-1\">→ After: The &quot;forgotten&quot; test-material change<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p224-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"action spoken","w":"horikita-suzune chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae the  forgotten  test material change previously told class d that age of discovery material would be on the midterm   one week later the class learns from class c that the test s material is different  when horikita confronts her in the faculty room  admits the change without a flicker of emotion  claims forgetfulness  and hands over handwritten textbook page numbers for the revised scope   that s right  the test s topics changed last friday  sorry  i must ve forgotten to inform you   thanks to you  horikita san  i was able to correct my mistake  i m grateful to all of you  that s all  thank you   i don t think so  you still have one week  if you use that study time wisely  it should be easy  right   shooing them out  total indifference to the gravity of the blunder   even if you stay  nothing will change  you understand that  don t you  doesn t apologize as they leave  the other teachers  except a smiling hoshinomiya  don t react at all   ayanokoji concludes it was no accident  ayanokoji s reading  her earlier unshakable certainty that  there was a way for us to pass this test  was the tell   the old test pipeline is the institutional escape hatch she hinted at  cold professional exchange  horikita withdraws the group rationally rather than escalating","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p224-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">The &quot;forgotten&quot; test-material change<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.224–226<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"horikita-suzune.html\">Horikita Suzune<\/a><\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“That’s right. The test’s topics changed last Friday. Sorry, I must’ve forgotten to inform you.”<span class=\"pg\">p.225<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“Thanks to you, Horikita-san, I was able to correct my mistake. I’m grateful to all of you. That’s all. Thank you.”<span class=\"pg\">p.225<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“I don’t think so. You still have one week. If you use that study time wisely, it should be easy. Right?” (shooing them out; total indifference to the gravity of the blunder)<span class=\"pg\">p.225<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>6 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Previously told Class D that Age of Discovery material would be on the midterm — one week later the class learns from Class C that the test&#x27;s material is different.<span class=\"pg\">p.224<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> When Horikita confronts her in the faculty room, admits the change without a flicker of emotion, claims forgetfulness, and hands over handwritten textbook page numbers for the revised scope.<span class=\"pg\">p.225<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Even if you stay, nothing will change. You understand that, don’t you?”<span class=\"pg\">p.226<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Doesn&#x27;t apologize as they leave; the other teachers (except a smiling Hoshinomiya) don&#x27;t react at all — Ayanokōji concludes it was no accident.<span class=\"pg\">p.226<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> Ayanokōji&#x27;s reading: her earlier unshakable certainty that &quot;there was a way for us to pass this test&quot; was the tell — the old-test pipeline is the institutional escape hatch she hinted at.<span class=\"pg\">p.234<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Horikita Suzune<\/b> Cold professional exchange; Horikita withdraws the group rationally rather than escalating.<span class=\"pg\">p.225<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p152-1\">← Before: Compass-stab wake-up &amp; discipline effects<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p240-1\">→ After: Exam-day opener &amp; the vacation reward<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p240-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"action spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae exam day opener   the vacation reward opens exam day with bold satisfaction at full attendance   there are no absences today  it appears everyone is present   that s the first hurdle for you leftovers  are there any questions  announces the institutional reward structure between papers   if anyone stumbles here  the other tests will be an uphill battle  quite frankly  you ll take this midterm and the final exam in july  if no one fails either test  you ll be rewarded with a vacation during your summer break   that s right  a dream vacation on an island surrounded by the brilliant blue sea","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p240-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Exam-day opener &amp; the vacation reward<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.240<\/span><\/h3><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“There are no absences today. It appears everyone is present.”<span class=\"pg\">p.240<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“That’s the first hurdle for you leftovers. Are there any questions?”<span class=\"pg\">p.240<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“If anyone stumbles here, the other tests will be an uphill battle, quite frankly. You’ll take this midterm and the final exam in July. If no one fails either test, you’ll be rewarded with a vacation during your summer break.”<span class=\"pg\">p.240<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>3 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Opens exam day with bold satisfaction at full attendance.<span class=\"pg\">p.240<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Announces the institutional reward structure between papers.<span class=\"pg\">p.240<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “That’s right. A dream vacation on an island surrounded by the brilliant blue sea.”<span class=\"pg\">p.240<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p224-1\">← Before: The &quot;forgotten&quot; test-material change<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p246-1\">→ After: The red line, the rounding kill &amp; the hallway covenant<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p246-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"action spoken","w":"ayanokouji-kiyotaka horikita-suzune chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae the red line  the rounding kill   the hallway covenant posts the results sheet with genuine surprise at the class s performance   more than ten perfect scores across math  japanese  and social studies   honestly  i m impressed  i didn t think that you d score so well  many students tied with perfect scores in mathematics  japanese  and social studies  more than ten of you  actually  draws a red line directly above sudou s name the moment celebration starts   you failed  sudou   when did anyone say that thirty two points was a passing grade   say whatever you want  it won t matter  this is the undeniable truth  you had to score at least a forty to pass the midterm exam  in other words  you were just one point short  you were so close  writes the class pass grade formula on the board  79 6   2   39 8   we set a passing grade for each individual class  just as we did for the last test  we calculated that number by dividing the average score by two  that s how we arrived at our answer  runs expulsion processing without emotion  withdrawal form  legal guardian required   then hands sudou s english sheet to hirata on request  i was expecting that you d protest   he s being expelled  he got a failing grade     there are no mistakes   hirata s verification  faces horikita s decimal inconsistency challenge and closes it with the true rule   we didn t simply omit the decimal when we calculated the passing grade  we rounded the numbers up or down  on the last test  we rounded down to thirty two points  and on this  we rounded up to forty  there s your answer   you should have noticed that we rounded the numbers  but to hold on to that possibility  well  too bad  at any rate  first period will be starting soon  i ll be going  waits at the faculty room window  almost as if she were waiting for someone  engages ayanokoji s fairness inquiry   if you re asking for my personal opinion  then  no  of course not  the world isn t fair  not even the slightest bit   but it s an excellent example of how unfair the world is  in fact  you could call it a microcosm of our unfair society   that s unfortunate  what you ve said isn t wrong  but i can t allow you to do that  sudou will be expelled  that decision cannot be overturned at this stage  give up   the hedge ak pounces on   at this stage  reveals full knowledge of the old test operation   including that ak personally contacted the third year   and openly praises it   ayanokouji  i personally hold you in rather high regard  i ve thought so since assigning this test  obtaining the old test problems was certainly one correct solution  you distributed the old test problems to everyone in class and raised the average scores  i have to praise such a logical decision  honestly  you did very well  when ak proposes buying one test point  bursts out laughing   then names the price   ha ha ha ha ha  that s a rather interesting idea  you really are a different kind of student  i never imagined you d try to buy points   seeing as how this is a special occasion  i ll sell a test point for the exceptional price of 100 000 points  accepts ak s and horikita s id cards together  sells one point for a combined 100 000 points and cancels sudou s expulsion on the spot   fine  i accept your deal  i ll sell you one point to apply to sudou s test  taking a combined total of 100 000 points from you both  as for the matter of sudou s expulsion  you can inform the class that s no longer the case   do you understand how talented ayanokouji is  at least somewhat   to horikita   no one from class d has ever been promoted before  the school has already labeled you defective and will coldly toss you aside  how will you accomplish your goal   well  i look forward to seeing what you do next  as your homeroom teacher  i ll be sure to watch over you with great attention and care  rule shaped cruelty with deliberate seams  enforces expulsion without mercy  yet her hedges  waits  and praise are an invitation to exploit the system she just demonstrated  full mutual awareness  she knows his every move  prices his audacity at 100 000  and formally becomes his invested homeroom sponsor  defeats her counter trap  then rewards her defective vs trash answer with visible approval","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p246-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">The red line, the rounding kill &amp; the hallway covenant<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.246–255<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"ayanokouji-kiyotaka.html\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/a> · <a href=\"horikita-suzune.html\">Horikita Suzune<\/a><\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“Honestly, I’m impressed. I didn’t think that you’d score so well. Many students tied with perfect scores in mathematics, Japanese, and social studies. More than ten of you, actually.”<span class=\"pg\">p.246<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“You failed, Sudou.”<span class=\"pg\">p.247<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“When did anyone say that thirty-two points was a passing grade?”<span class=\"pg\">p.247<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>27 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Posts the results sheet with genuine surprise at the class&#x27;s performance — more than ten perfect scores across math, Japanese, and social studies.<span class=\"pg\">p.246<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Draws a red line directly above Sudou&#x27;s name the moment celebration starts.<span class=\"pg\">p.247<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Say whatever you want, it won’t matter. This is the undeniable truth. You had to score at least a forty to pass the midterm exam. In other words, you were just one point short. You were so close.”<span class=\"pg\">p.247<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Writes the class pass-grade formula on the board: 79.6 ÷ 2 = 39.8.<span class=\"pg\">p.247<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “We set a passing grade for each individual class, just as we did for the last test. We calculated that number by dividing the average score by two. That’s how we arrived at our answer.”<span class=\"pg\">p.247<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Runs expulsion processing without emotion: withdrawal form, legal guardian required — then hands Sudou&#x27;s English sheet to Hirata on request (&quot;I was expecting that you&#x27;d protest&quot;).<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “He’s being expelled. He got a failing grade.” / “There…are no mistakes.” (Hirata&#x27;s verification)<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Faces Horikita&#x27;s decimal inconsistency challenge and closes it with the true rule.<span class=\"pg\">p.249–250<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “We didn’t simply omit the decimal when we calculated the passing grade. We rounded the numbers up or down. On the last test, we rounded down to thirty-two points, and on this, we rounded up to forty. There’s your answer.”<span class=\"pg\">p.250<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “You should have noticed that we rounded the numbers, but to hold on to that possibility… Well, too bad. At any rate, first period will be starting soon. I’ll be going.”<span class=\"pg\">p.250<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Waits at the faculty-room window &quot;almost as if she were waiting for someone&quot;; engages Ayanokōji&#x27;s fairness inquiry.<span class=\"pg\">p.250–252<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “If you’re asking for my personal opinion, then, no, of course not. The world isn’t fair, not even the slightest bit.”<span class=\"pg\">p.251<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “But it’s an excellent example of how unfair the world is. In fact, you could call it a microcosm of our unfair society.”<span class=\"pg\">p.251<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “That’s unfortunate. What you’ve said isn’t wrong, but I can’t allow you to do that. Sudou will be expelled. That decision cannot be overturned at this stage. Give up.” (the hedge AK pounces on: &quot;at this stage&quot;)<span class=\"pg\">p.252<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Reveals full knowledge of the old-test operation — including that AK personally contacted the third-year — and openly praises it.<span class=\"pg\">p.252<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Ayanokouji, I personally hold you in rather high regard. I’ve thought so since assigning this test. Obtaining the old test problems was certainly one correct solution… you distributed the old test problems to everyone in class and raised the average scores. I have to praise such a logical decision. Honestly, you did very well.”<span class=\"pg\">p.252<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> When AK proposes buying one test point, bursts out laughing — then names the price.<span class=\"pg\">p.253<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Ha ha ha ha ha! That’s a rather interesting idea. You really are a different kind of student. I never imagined you’d try to buy points.”<span class=\"pg\">p.253<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Seeing as how this is a special occasion, I’ll sell a test point for the exceptional price of 100,000 points.”<span class=\"pg\">p.253<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Accepts AK&#x27;s and Horikita&#x27;s ID cards together: sells ONE point for a combined 100,000 points and cancels Sudou&#x27;s expulsion on the spot.<span class=\"pg\">p.253–254<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Fine. I accept your deal. I’ll sell you one point to apply to Sudou’s test, taking a combined total of 100,000 points from you both. As for the matter of Sudou’s expulsion, you can inform the class that’s no longer the case.”<span class=\"pg\">p.254<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Do you understand how talented Ayanokouji is? At least somewhat?” (to Horikita)<span class=\"pg\">p.254<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “No one from Class D has ever been promoted before. The school has already labeled you defective and will coldly toss you aside. How will you accomplish your goal?”<span class=\"pg\">p.254<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Well, I look forward to seeing what you do next. As your homeroom teacher, I’ll be sure to watch over you with great attention and care.”<span class=\"pg\">p.255<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> Rule-shaped cruelty with deliberate seams: enforces expulsion without mercy, yet her hedges, waits, and praise are an invitation to exploit the system she just demonstrated.<span class=\"pg\">p.246–255<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/b> Full mutual awareness: she knows his every move, prices his audacity at 100,000, and formally becomes his invested homeroom sponsor.<span class=\"pg\">p.252–255<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Horikita Suzune<\/b> Defeats her counter-trap, then rewards her defective-vs-trash answer with visible approval.<span class=\"pg\">p.249–255<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v1-p240-1\">← Before: Exam-day opener &amp; the vacation reward<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p16-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 2  2 1  july 1  the point board  and a deposit with a rider she  strode into the classroom as the homeroom bell rang  to a class d more raucous than usual  the first payout day since enrollment   good morning  everyone  you all seem more restless than usual today   oh  so that s why you re all so restless   don t jump to conclusions  listen to what i have to say first  you re correct  ike  you have all worked harder than ever before  i recognize that  naturally  the school understands full well how you all feel  puts a paper listing the month s point totals on the board  class a at the top  rather than letting the class read the app s numbers unexplained   well then  without further ado  here are this month s point totals   well  this time there was a little trouble  the first year students  point distribution has been delayed  i m sorry  but you ll have to wait a bit longer   don t blame me  this was the school s decision  there s nothing i can do about it  once the trouble has been resolved  you ll receive your points  if there are still points left  that is  the same method as entrance day  now in maintenance mode  buy the room s acknowledgment first  you re correct  ike  then deliver the bad news  then assign it to the school and refuse responsibility   closing on a conditional no student asks her to unpack  ayanokoji is the only one who hears the rider as a warning   there was deeper meaning behind chabashira sensei s words  ak s memory of her entrance day line is what taught the class to spend  he  understood that our homeroom teacher  chabashira sensei  hadn t been kidding when she told us   at this school  you can buy anything with your points","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p16-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.2 §2.1 (July 1): the point board, and a deposit with a rider<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.16–18<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">She &quot;strode into the classroom as the homeroom bell rang&quot; to a Class D more raucous than usual, the first payout day since enrollment. Puts a paper listing the month&#x27;s point totals on the board, Class A at the top, rather than letting the class read the app&#x27;s numbers unexplained.<\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“Good morning, everyone. You all seem more restless than usual today.”<span class=\"pg\">p.16<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“Oh, so that’s why you’re all so restless?”<span class=\"pg\">p.16<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“Don’t jump to conclusions. Listen to what I have to say first. You’re correct, Ike. You have all worked harder than ever before. I recognize that. Naturally, the school understands full well how you all feel.”<span class=\"pg\">p.16–17<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>6 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Well then. Without further ado, here are this month’s point totals.”<span class=\"pg\">p.17<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Well, this time there was a little trouble. The first-year students’ point distribution has been delayed. I’m sorry, but you’ll have to wait a bit longer,”<span class=\"pg\">p.18<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Don’t blame me. This was the school’s decision, there’s nothing I can do about it. Once the trouble has been resolved, you’ll receive your points. If there are still points left, that is.”<span class=\"pg\">p.18<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> The same method as entrance day, now in maintenance mode: buy the room&#x27;s acknowledgment first (&quot;You’re correct, Ike&quot;), then deliver the bad news, then assign it to the school and refuse responsibility — closing on a conditional no student asks her to unpack.<span class=\"pg\">p.16–18<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Backstory<\/span> Ayanokōji is the only one who hears the rider as a warning: &quot;There was deeper meaning behind Chabashira-sensei’s words.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.18<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Backstory<\/span> AK&#x27;s memory of her entrance-day line is what taught the class to spend: he &quot;understood that our homeroom teacher, Chabashira-sensei, hadn’t been kidding when she told us, &#x27;At this school, you can buy anything with your points.&#x27;&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.16<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p22-1\">→ After: Ch.2 §2.3: collecting Sudou for the faculty room<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p22-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"spoken","w":"sudou-ken chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 2  2 3  collecting sudou for the faculty room  sudou  i have to talk to you about something  come to the faculty room   i ve already spoken with the advisor  you don t have to come with me if you don t want to  but you will face the consequences later   that all depends on you  the longer you stand there  the more time you waste  she removes the excuse before she makes the request   the club advisor has already been told  so his  i ve got basketball practice now  buys nothing  refuses to estimate how long it will take  that all depends on you  first direct contact since she cancelled his expulsion at the midterm  private summons  no audience in front of the class  and he  clicked his tongue  and followed  her out","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p22-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.2 §2.3: collecting Sudou for the faculty room<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.22<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"sudou-ken.html\">Sudō Ken<\/a><\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“Sudou. I have to talk to you about something. Come to the faculty room,”<span class=\"pg\">p.22<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“I’ve already spoken with the advisor. You don’t have to come with me if you don’t want to, but you will face the consequences later.”<span class=\"pg\">p.22<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“That all depends on you. The longer you stand there, the more time you waste.”<span class=\"pg\">p.22<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>2 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> She removes the excuse before she makes the request — the club advisor has already been told, so his &quot;I’ve got basketball practice now&quot; buys nothing. Refuses to estimate how long it will take (&quot;That all depends on you&quot;).<span class=\"pg\">p.22<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Sudō Ken<\/b> First direct contact since she cancelled his expulsion at the midterm: private summons, no audience in front of the class, and he &quot;clicked his tongue, and followed&quot; her out.<span class=\"pg\">p.22<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p16-1\">← Before: Ch.2 §2.1 (July 1): the point board, and a deposit with a rider<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p40-1\">→ After: Ch.3 open (July 2 homeroom): she makes Sudou&#x27;s fight public<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p40-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 3 open  july 2 homeroom  she makes sudou s fight public about to leave homeroom  she  assaulted us with one of her infamously brief and off hand announcements  naming sudou and class c in front of the whole class   i have an announcement for you all  there was a bit of trouble the other day  an incident between the student sitting there  sudou  and some students from class c  in short  there was a fight  ak s read of the delivery   her face was so devoid of emotion or interest when she spoke that there was a certain serene beauty to it  she inserted no personal bias whatsoever when she addressed the class  and explained the situation from a position of neutrality   the complaint came from class c  they claim that the fight was one sided  however  when we spoke to the accused  sudou said that their claims were false  he insists that the students class c called him over and started the fight   so in other words  we don t yet know the truth  therefore  we have put our decision on hold for now  our response  and the punishment  will come when we find out who was at fault   so speaks the accused  but i wouldn t say that you have a high level of credibility right now  if there is an eyewitness  as sudou seems to believe  then the situation may change  if anyone here witnessed the fight  please raise your hand   chabashira sensei continued talking in a robotic  flat voice  no students raised their hands in response to her question   too bad  sudou  it appears there are no witnesses in this class   in order to check for witnesses  every teacher will inform their classes of the details of this incident   anyway  that is all  we ll make our final judgment next tuesday  taking into account any eyewitnesses and evidence  with that  let s end homeroom for the day  she destroys sudou s containment plan as a side effect of doing her job neutrally  no editorialising  no protection  and the school wide notification she volunteers is the step that turns a class matter into a cohort matter  ak   the school probably had no say in the matter  the disciplinary process stated on the page  accusation and defence both recorded  decision held pending eyewitness or evidence  judgment date fixed  next tuesday  and every class informed so witnesses can be found outside class d  she leaves and  sudou followed right after her    ak s read  he stayed out of the room rather than lose his temper at his classmates","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p40-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.3 open (July 2 homeroom): she makes Sudou&#x27;s fight public<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.40–41<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">About to leave homeroom, she &quot;assaulted us with one of her infamously brief and off-hand announcements&quot;, naming Sudou and Class C in front of the whole class. &quot;Chabashira-sensei continued talking in a robotic, flat voice. No students raised their hands in response to her question.&quot; She leaves and &quot;Sudou followed right after her&quot; — AK&#x27;s read: he stayed out of the room rather than lose his temper at his classmates.<\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“I have an announcement for you all. There was a bit of trouble the other day, an incident between the student sitting there, Sudou, and some students from Class C. In short, there was a fight.”<span class=\"pg\">p.40<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“The complaint came from Class C. They claim that the fight was one-sided. However, when we spoke to the accused, Sudou said that their claims were false. He insists that the students Class C called him over and started the…”<span class=\"pg\">p.40<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“So in other words, we don’t yet know the truth. Therefore, we have put our decision on hold for now. Our response, and the punishment, will come when we find out who was at fault.”<span class=\"pg\">p.41<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>8 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> AK&#x27;s read of the delivery: &quot;Her face was so devoid of emotion or interest when she spoke that there was a certain serene beauty to it. She inserted no personal bias whatsoever when she addressed the class, and explained the situation from a position of neutrality.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.40<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “The complaint came from Class C. They claim that the fight was one-sided. However, when we spoke to the accused, Sudou said that their claims were false. He insists that the students Class C called him over and started the fight.”<span class=\"pg\">p.40<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “So speaks the accused, but I wouldn’t say that you have a high level of credibility right now. If there is an eyewitness, as Sudou seems to believe, then the situation may change. If anyone here witnessed the fight, please raise your hand.”<span class=\"pg\">p.41<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Too bad, Sudou. It appears there are no witnesses in this class.”<span class=\"pg\">p.41<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “In order to check for witnesses, every teacher will inform their classes of the details of this incident.”<span class=\"pg\">p.41<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Anyway, that is all. We’ll make our final judgment next Tuesday, taking into account any eyewitnesses and evidence. With that, let’s end homeroom for the day.”<span class=\"pg\">p.41<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> She destroys Sudou&#x27;s containment plan as a side effect of doing her job neutrally: no editorialising, no protection, and the school-wide notification she volunteers is the step that turns a class matter into a cohort matter. AK: &quot;The school probably had no say in the matter.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.40–41<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> The disciplinary process stated on the page: accusation and defence both recorded, decision held pending eyewitness or evidence, judgment date fixed (next Tuesday), and every class informed so witnesses can be found outside Class D.<span class=\"pg\">p.41<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p22-1\">← Before: Ch.2 §2.3: collecting Sudou for the faculty room<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p163-1\">→ After: Ch.5 §5.2: the teacher who will not take the witness on trust<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p163-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 5  5 2  the teacher who will not take the witness on trust the faculty room  after homeroom  at the class s request  ayanokoji hangs back   a witness  for sudou s case   i understand what you re said  however  i can t simply accept what you re telling me   sakura  why are you testifying now  when i reported the issue during homeroom  you didn t step forward  it s not as though you were absent that day  right   you re not good at talking with other people  and yet you ve decided to testify now  isn t that strange   i m speaking to sakura right now  her interrogation of a frightened witness reduces the girl physically   sakura hunched and shrank away  much like a frog cornered by a snake  the narrator adds that she  should have understood what kind of girl sakura was  and that speaking up at all  had made great progress    the method is exact and costly   i see  so  then you mustered up the courage to come forward   i see  well if you are a witness as you say  naturally i m obligated to relay that information to the school  however  while the school will listen to the whole story  sudou may not be declared innocent   are you really the witness  sakura  that s what i m getting at  i think that it may be a lie made up by class d because the students are afraid of receiving a negative evaluation   horrible  if you really had witnessed an event  you should have come forward on the first day  it s natural to feel suspicious when someone steps up just in the nick of time  considering that the witness is from class d  it s doubly suspicious  any reasonable person would have doubts  don t you think so  conveniently  a student from the same class happened to be in an infrequently visited building and happened to witness the entire event   however  a witness is a witness  i cannot determine if she s lying  so for the time being  i will accept her testimony  so  sakura  i will ask for you to join us on the day of deliberation  i understand that you do not like to associate with others  but can you do this   if you don t like it  you do have the option to withdraw  also  we ll tell sudou that he will be participating in the deliberation   if sudou himself consents  i will approve it  however  we cannot allow that many people  only a maximum of two may sit in on the deliberation  please think on that carefully  she tests the witness by making her imagine the room   as if she were testing the girl  the narrator s judgment of her method  kept as his   as our teacher  chabashira sensei should have understood what kind of girl sakura was","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p163-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.5 §5.2: the teacher who will not take the witness on trust<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.163–166<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">The faculty room, after homeroom, at the class&#x27;s request; Ayanokōji hangs back.<\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;A witness? For Sudou’s case?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.163<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;I understand what you’re said. However, I can’t simply accept what you’re telling me,&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.163<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;Sakura, why are you testifying now? When I reported the issue during homeroom, you didn’t step forward. It’s not as though you were absent that day, right?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.163<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>12 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;You’re not good at talking with other people, and yet you’ve decided to testify now? Isn’t that strange?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.164<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;I’m speaking to Sakura right now.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.164<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Limit<\/span> Her interrogation of a frightened witness reduces the girl physically: &quot;Sakura hunched and shrank away, much like a frog cornered by a snake.&quot; The narrator adds that she &quot;should have understood what kind of girl Sakura was&quot; and that speaking up at all &quot;had made great progress&quot; — the method is exact and costly.<span class=\"pg\">p.164<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;I see. So, then you mustered up the courage to come forward?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.164<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;I see. Well if you are a witness as you say, naturally I’m obligated to relay that information to the school. However, while the school will listen to the whole story, Sudou may not be declared innocent.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.164<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Are you really the witness, Sakura? That’s what I’m getting at. I think that it may be a lie made up by Class D because the students are afraid of receiving a negative evaluation.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.164<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Horrible? If you really had witnessed an event, you should have come forward on the first day. It’s natural to feel suspicious when someone steps up just in the nick of time. Considering that the witness is from Class D, it’s doubly suspicious. Any reasonable person would have doubts. Don’t you think so? Conveniently, a student from the same class happened to be in an infrequently visited building and happened to witness the entire event?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.164<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;However, a witness is a witness. I cannot determine if she’s lying, so for the time being, I will accept her testimony. So, Sakura, I will ask for you to join us on the day of deliberation. I understand that you do not like to associate with others, but can you do this?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.165<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;If you don’t like it, you do have the option to withdraw. Also, we’ll tell Sudou that he will be participating in the deliberation.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.165<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;If Sudou himself consents, I will approve it. However, we cannot allow that many people. Only a maximum of two may sit in on the deliberation. Please think on that carefully.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.165<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> She tests the witness by making her imagine the room: &quot;as if she were testing the girl.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.164–165<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The narrator&#x27;s judgment of her method, kept as his: &quot;As our teacher, Chabashira-sensei should have understood what kind of girl Sakura was.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.164<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p40-1\">← Before: Ch.3 open (July 2 homeroom): she makes Sudou&#x27;s fight public<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p192-1\">→ After: Ch.6 open: the rules she releases one at a time<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p192-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 6 open  the rules she releases one at a time the faculty room before the hearing  hoshinomiya tries to tag along and chabashira pushes her back inside   what are you doing this time   whenever you sneak out  that s around the time i start to feel suspicious   of course you can t  you know that outsiders cannot participate   well then  shall we be going   of course not  this school does have rather complicated rules  but in cases such as these a settlement is reached between the homeroom teacher of the class in question  the concerned parties  and the student council  the venue is disclosed last  after horikita has already agreed to attend  the narrator calls it her habit of revealing  an important detail right at the last minute   if you want to stop  now s the time  horikita  the fourth floor student council room  she enters first and sets the proceedings in motion   sorry we re late   have you already met   this is sakagami sensei  the homeroom teacher for class c  now then   this is the student council president   well then  i would now like to discuss the violent incident that occurred last tuesday with the members of the student council  the involved parties  and their homeroom teachers  you may begin the proceedings  student council secretary tachibana   so  this is all by chance  the question is aimed at the president s claim of attending by choice  he does not answer it","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p192-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.6 open: the rules she releases one at a time<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.192–193<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">The faculty room before the hearing; Hoshinomiya tries to tag along and Chabashira pushes her back inside. The fourth-floor student council room; she enters first and sets the proceedings in motion.<\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;What are you doing this time?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.192<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;Whenever you sneak out, that’s around the time I start to feel suspicious.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.192<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;Of course you can’t. You know that outsiders cannot participate.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.192<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>11 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Well then, shall we be going?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.192<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Of course not. This school does have rather complicated rules, but in cases such as these a settlement is reached between the homeroom teacher of the class in question, the concerned parties, and the student council.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.192<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The venue is disclosed last, after Horikita has already agreed to attend; the narrator calls it her habit of revealing &quot;an important detail right at the last minute.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.192<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;If you want to stop, now’s the time, Horikita.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.192<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Sorry we’re late.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.193<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Have you already met?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.193<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;This is Sakagami-sensei, the homeroom teacher for Class C. Now then.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.193<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;This is the student council president.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.193<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Well then, I would now like to discuss the violent incident that occurred last Tuesday with the members of the student council, the involved parties, and their homeroom teachers. You may begin the proceedings, student council secretary Tachibana.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.193<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;So, this is all by chance?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.193<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The question is aimed at the president’s claim of attending by choice; he does not answer it.<span class=\"pg\">p.193<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p163-1\">← Before: Ch.5 §5.2: the teacher who will not take the witness on trust<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p199-1\">→ After: Ch.6 §6.1: the witness she dismisses<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p199-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 6  6 1  the witness she dismisses  why are you in such a hurry  sakagami sensei  when sakagami moves to strike the witness  she agrees with him instead of resisting   you d think that chabashira sensei would ally herself with class d  but that didn t seem to be the case   you re certainly right that this is a waste of time  so i suppose we have no choice  you can come down now  sakura  the order is what forces sakura s outburst  the chapter does not say whether chabashira expected it  and the narrator records only that she  ordered sakura to leave  almost as if she d lost interest","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p199-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.6 §6.1: the witness she dismisses<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.199–200<\/span><\/h3><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;Why are you in such a hurry, Sakagami-sensei?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.199<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;You’re certainly right that this is a waste of time, so I suppose we have no choice. You can come down now, Sakura.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.200<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>2 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> When Sakagami moves to strike the witness, she agrees with him instead of resisting: &quot;You’d think that Chabashira-sensei would ally herself with Class D, but that didn’t seem to be the case.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.199–200<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Limit<\/span> The order is what forces Sakura’s outburst; the chapter does not say whether Chabashira expected it, and the narrator records only that she &quot;ordered Sakura to leave, almost as if she’d lost interest.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.200<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p192-1\">← Before: Ch.6 open: the rules she releases one at a time<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p205-1\">→ After: Ch.6 §6.1: the compromise she will not refuse<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p205-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"spoken","w":"horikita-suzune chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 6  6 1  the compromise she will not refuse  compromise   we seem to have already reached a logical conclusion  there s no reason to refuse sakagami sensei s proposal  she treats the settlement as arithmetic rather than advocacy  the proposal is  certainly a reasonable compromise  and she says so in front of her own student   if this matter required some extra time before retrial  then the student council president would have offered a sufficient grace period  in other words  the amount of time granted should be enough for this case  extensions are only offered under special circumstances  the disciplinary settlement is heard by the two homeroom teachers  the concerned students and the student council  the retrial window is fixed by the council and cannot be extended on request  she warns horikita she can still withdraw  then closes the only procedural door horikita asks to reopen","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p205-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.6 §6.1: the compromise she will not refuse<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.205–209<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"horikita-suzune.html\">Horikita Suzune<\/a><\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;Compromise?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.205<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;We seem to have already reached a logical conclusion. There’s no reason to refuse Sakagami-sensei’s proposal,&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.206<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;If this matter required some extra time before retrial, then the student council president would have offered a sufficient grace period. In other words, the amount of time granted should be enough for this case. Extensions are…<span class=\"pg\">p.209<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>4 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> She treats the settlement as arithmetic rather than advocacy: the proposal is &quot;certainly a reasonable compromise,&quot; and she says so in front of her own student.<span class=\"pg\">p.206<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;If this matter required some extra time before retrial, then the student council president would have offered a sufficient grace period. In other words, the amount of time granted should be enough for this case. Extensions are only offered under special circumstances,&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.209<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> The disciplinary settlement is heard by the two homeroom teachers, the concerned students and the student council; the retrial window is fixed by the council and cannot be extended on request.<span class=\"pg\">p.192, 209<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Horikita Suzune<\/b> She warns Horikita she can still withdraw, then closes the only procedural door Horikita asks to reopen.<span class=\"pg\">p.192, 209<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p199-1\">← Before: Ch.6 §6.1: the witness she dismisses<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p243-1\">→ After: Ch.7 §7.5: the empty seat and the unexpected ending<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p243-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 7  7 5  the empty seat and the unexpected ending she arrives with sakagami for the retrial and finds ayanokoji s seat empty   what happened to ayanokouji  horikita   not participating  she stares at the vacant seat  as if the proceedings would be meaningless without him   you re withdrawing your complaint  she chuckles with  a thin smile  as if she found something amusing   oh  excuse me  i was just surprised because i hadn t foreseen this  i figured we would argue all day until one side collapsed  or until we could propose an acceptable compromise  however  unbelievably  they ve said that they want to withdraw the complaint  she confirms the practical result to both sides  no suspension for sudo  no punishment for class c  with this incident as the example to remember next time   of course  the same goes for the students in class c  naturally  youthful zeal is a good quality  however  the next time you think about causing problems  you should remember this incident as an example  do not forget  okay","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p243-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.7 §7.5: the empty seat and the unexpected ending<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.243–246<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">She arrives with Sakagami for the retrial and finds Ayanokōji’s seat empty.<\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;What happened to Ayanokouji, Horikita?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.243<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;Not participating?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.243<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;You’re withdrawing your complaint?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.244<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>5 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> She stares at the vacant seat &quot;as if the proceedings would be meaningless without him.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.243<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> She chuckles with &quot;a thin smile, as if she found something amusing.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.244<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Oh, excuse me. I was just surprised because I hadn’t foreseen this. I figured we would argue all day until one side collapsed, or until we could propose an acceptable compromise. However, unbelievably, they’ve said that they want to withdraw the complaint.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.244<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> She confirms the practical result to both sides: no suspension for Sudō, no punishment for Class C, with this incident as the example to remember next time.<span class=\"pg\">p.246<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Of course. The same goes for the students in Class C, naturally. Youthful zeal is a good quality. However, the next time you think about causing problems, you should remember this incident as an example. Do not forget. Okay?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.246<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p205-1\">← Before: Ch.6 §6.1: the compromise she will not refuse<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p246-1\">→ After: Ch.7 §7.5: what she says to Sudō<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p246-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 7  7 5  what she says to sudo  i m glad  sudou   personally  i think you should have been punished   the reason this incident occurred at all is because of your behavior  who told the truth and who lied is trivial  it s important that you don t let this kind of thing happen again  you do understand that  yes   however  admitting your own faults isn t  cool  so even if you acknowledge that your personality was partly to blame  you act tough  you get stronger  that s fine  however  if you act like that  you won t be able to make real friends  eventually  horikita will abandon you  she ll leave   there s strength in admitting your mistakes  sudou  the page records it as the first time she has tried to reach out to one of her students as a homeroom teacher   you shouldn t make promises so casually  you ll cause trouble soon again   what do you think  horikita  do you think that sudou will become a model student","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p246-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.7 §7.5: what she says to Sudō<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.246–247<\/span><\/h3><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;I’m glad, Sudou,&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.246<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;Personally, I think you should have been punished,&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.246<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;The reason this incident occurred at all is because of your behavior. Who told the truth and who lied is trivial. It’s important that you don’t let this kind of thing happen again. You do understand that, yes?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.246<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>5 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;However, admitting your own faults isn’t ‘cool.’ So even if you acknowledge that your personality was partly to blame, you act tough. You get stronger. That’s fine. However, if you act like that, you won’t be able to make real friends. Eventually, Horikita will abandon you. She’ll leave.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.246–247<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;There’s strength in admitting your mistakes, Sudou.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.247<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> The page records it as the first time she has tried to reach out to one of her students as a homeroom teacher.<span class=\"pg\">p.247<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;You shouldn’t make promises so casually. You’ll cause trouble soon again.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.247<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;What do you think, Horikita? Do you think that Sudou will become a model student?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.247<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p243-1\">← Before: Ch.7 §7.5: the empty seat and the unexpected ending<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p248-1\">→ After: Ch.7 §7.5: the private lesson about Ayanokōji<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p248-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 7  7 5  the private lesson about ayanokoji  so  what methods did you use  horikita   don t try to dodge the question  they wouldn t withdraw their complaint without a reason  would they   so it s a secret  hmm  well  allow me to change the question  who came up with the strategy that defeated class c   ayanokouji isn t here  so i m a little curious   i see  so you recognize that  hmm   his potential  hmm   there are various things to consider  but if you want to reach class a  i ll give you one bit of advice   the students in class d all have some kind of defect  more or less  to borrow an expression used by others in this school  class d is an assemblage of people that could be called  defective products  you already understand this quite well though  don t you   well  what do you think is ayanokouji s defect   oh  and what is it   he dislikes trouble  hmm  is that what you perceive when you look at ayanokouji   well  horikita  let s try to learn as much as we possibly can about the boy called ayanokouji  without delay  shall we  otherwise  it ll be too late  you already seem to have fallen into ayanokouji s trap   why do you think ayanokouji purposefully got scores of 50 points across on the board on his entrance examination  why do you think ayanokouji is helping you  why do you think that ayanokouji doesn t put himself forward as a superior student  despite having superior ability  is ayanokouji kiyotaka really someone who  dislikes trouble   in my personal opinion  ayanokouji is the most defective student in class d   higher functioning products are more difficult to handle  if you misunderstand how to handle him  the class might be completely destroyed in short order   get to know the person called ayanokouji  what is he thinking  what focal point does he base his actions around  what is his fatal flaw  there s definitely an answer there  she says nothing more  and the page gives no reason for the warning beyond horikita s own unanswered question  her usual manner is  unaware of and unconcerned with her class  which is why the session itself is the surprise","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p248-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.7 §7.5: the private lesson about Ayanokōji<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.248–251<\/span><\/h3><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;So. What methods did you use, Horikita?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;Don’t try to dodge the question. They wouldn’t withdraw their complaint without a reason, would they?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;So it’s a secret, hmm? Well, allow me to change the question. Who came up with the strategy that defeated Class C?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>15 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Ayanokouji isn’t here, so I’m a little curious.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;I see. So you recognize that, hmm?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;His potential, hmm?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;There are various things to consider. But if you want to reach Class A, I’ll give you one bit of advice.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;The students in Class D all have some kind of defect, more or less. To borrow an expression used by others in this school, Class D is an assemblage of people that could be called ‘defective products.’ You already understand this quite well though, don’t you?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Well, what do you think is Ayanokouji’s defect?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Oh? And what is it?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;He dislikes trouble, hmm? Is that what you perceive when you look at Ayanokouji?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.249–250<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Well, Horikita. Let’s try to learn as much as we possibly can about the boy called Ayanokouji, without delay, shall we? Otherwise, it’ll be too late. You already seem to have fallen into Ayanokouji’s trap.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.250<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Why do you think Ayanokouji purposefully got scores of 50 points across on the board on his entrance examination? Why do you think Ayanokouji is helping you? Why do you think that Ayanokouji doesn’t put himself forward as a superior student, despite having superior ability? Is Ayanokouji Kiyotaka really someone who ‘dislikes trouble’?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.250<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;In my personal opinion, Ayanokouji is the most defective student in Class D.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.250<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Higher functioning products are more difficult to handle. If you misunderstand how to handle him, the class might be completely destroyed in short order.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.250<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> &quot;Get to know the person called Ayanokouji. What is he thinking? What focal point does he base his actions around? What is his fatal flaw? There’s definitely an answer there.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.250<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> She says nothing more, and the page gives no reason for the warning beyond Horikita’s own unanswered question.<span class=\"pg\">p.251<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> Her usual manner is &quot;unaware of and unconcerned with her class,&quot; which is why the session itself is the surprise.<span class=\"pg\">p.251<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v2-p246-1\">← Before: Ch.7 §7.5: what she says to Sudō<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p10-1","v":"y1v3","y":1,"o":3,"k":"inner narration","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 1  cold open in her pov  the icarus gamble the volume opens inside chabashira s first person with no setting and no interlocutor   a soliloquy that retells the myth of daedalus and icarus and then states why she provoked a certain young boy  the myth  her summary  daedalus  confined in a tower by king minos alongside his son icarus  fashions wings of bird feathers  thread and wax  the warning  if you fly too high  the sun will melt the wax holding the wings together  be careful  icarus forgets it  the wax melts  and he falls into the sea and dies  she frames her own predicament in classical myth    greek mythology deals abundantly with human frailty    treating her situation as a fable to be interpreted before it is confessed   for some reason  i thought of icarus  wings when standing before a certain young boy   considering what these past few months had brought  i could say that i made the comparison simply because he resembled icarus   but i immediately came to realize that the two boys were fundamentally different from one another  this boy possessed neither the bravery nor the arrogance of icarus   i had been pushed into a corner  i had no choice but to do it  cornered and out of means  she chose the provocation  incur the boy s divine wrath and turn his quiet rage onto herself   the die cannot be returned once cast  the gamble had already begun  soliloquy register  measured  literary  fatalistic   she narrates her own decision as a fable already decided  with no hedging and no appeal for sympathy  the boy is never named on these pages   only  a certain young boy     this boy  identity left open  not assigned from context","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p10-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.1: cold open in her POV, the Icarus gamble<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V3 · p.10–12<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">The volume opens inside Chabashira&#x27;s first person with no setting and no interlocutor — a soliloquy that retells the myth of Daedalus and Icarus and then states why she provoked a certain young boy.<\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q narration\">The myth, her summary: Daedalus, confined in a tower by King Minos alongside his son Icarus, fashions wings of bird feathers, thread and wax; the warning “If you fly too high, the sun will melt the wax holding the wings together.…<span class=\"pg\">p.10<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q inner\">&quot;For some reason, I thought of Icarus’ wings when standing before a certain young boy.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.10<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q inner\">&quot;Considering what these past few months had brought, I could say that I made the comparison simply because he resembled Icarus.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.10<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>8 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Narration<\/span> The myth, her summary: Daedalus, confined in a tower by King Minos alongside his son Icarus, fashions wings of bird feathers, thread and wax; the warning “If you fly too high, the sun will melt the wax holding the wings together. Be careful.”; Icarus forgets it, the wax melts, and he falls into the sea and dies.<span class=\"pg\">p.10<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> She frames her own predicament in classical myth — “Greek mythology deals abundantly with human frailty.” — treating her situation as a fable to be interpreted before it is confessed.<span class=\"pg\">p.10<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Inner voice<\/span> &quot;But I immediately came to realize that the two boys were fundamentally different from one another. This boy possessed neither the bravery nor the arrogance of Icarus.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.11<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Inner voice<\/span> &quot;I had been pushed into a corner. I had no choice but to do it.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.11<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Goal<\/span> Cornered and out of means, she chose the provocation: incur the boy&#x27;s divine wrath and turn his quiet rage onto herself.<span class=\"pg\">p.11<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Inner voice<\/span> &quot;The die cannot be returned once cast. The gamble had already begun.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.11<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Voice<\/span> Soliloquy register: measured, literary, fatalistic — she narrates her own decision as a fable already decided, with no hedging and no appeal for sympathy.<span class=\"pg\">p.10–11<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The boy is never named on these pages — only &quot;a certain young boy&quot; / &quot;this boy&quot;. Identity left open; not assigned from context.<span class=\"pg\">p.10–11<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p24-1\">→ After: Ch.2 §2.1: she runs the rules that sink the summer<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p24-1","v":"y1v3","y":1,"o":3,"k":"spoken","w":"hoshinomiya-chie ike-kanji chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 2  2 1  she runs the rules that sink the summer beach landing  she takes class d s roll call in the same jersey as the students   keeping on like that would be shameful  ike  so stop talking    and the world widening follow up   there are workplaces where there aren t any chairs in the office  and companies that decide salaries with dice rolls  the world is wider and deeper than you even know   in other words  you are ill equipped to distinguish between what is reality and what isn t     in my opinion  you re all equally worthless  delivers the penalty list verbatim from the manual   thirty for health retirement  twenty for pollution  five per absent student at roll call  disqualification plus private point forfeiture for inter class violence  assembles the cardboard toilet in front of the class and demonstrates the polymer sheet    five uses with one vinyl bag  explains the spot system  eight hour rights of exclusive possession  one provisional bonus point per occupation  fifty point penalty for using another class s spot  brushes off the eavesdropping complaint and the  fate  line  threatens a behavioural report  refuses the emergency bear question on process grounds   the test has already begun  i cannot answer any questions that might possibly influence your results  confirms the school  isn t involved in the test process at all    survival is the test","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p24-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.2 §2.1: she runs the rules that sink the summer<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V3 · p.24–39<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Beach landing; she takes Class D&#x27;s roll call in the same jersey as the students.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"hoshinomiya-chie.html\">Hoshinomiya Chie<\/a> · <a href=\"ike-kanji.html\">Ike Kanji<\/a><\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“Keeping on like that would be shameful, Ike, so stop talking.” — and the world-widening follow-up: “There are workplaces where there aren’t any chairs in the office, and companies that decide salaries with dice rolls. The world…”<span class=\"pg\">p.26<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“In other words, you are ill-equipped to distinguish between what is reality and what isn’t.” / “In my opinion, you’re all equally worthless.”<span class=\"pg\">p.26–27<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>7 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Keeping on like that would be shameful, Ike, so stop talking.” — and the world-widening follow-up: “There are workplaces where there aren’t any chairs in the office, and companies that decide salaries with dice rolls. The world is wider and deeper than you even know.”<span class=\"pg\">p.26<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> Delivers the penalty list verbatim from the manual — thirty for health retirement, twenty for pollution, five per absent student at roll call, disqualification plus private-point forfeiture for inter-class violence.<span class=\"pg\">p.31<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> Assembles the cardboard toilet in front of the class and demonstrates the polymer sheet — &quot;five uses with one vinyl bag.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.34<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> Explains the spot system: eight-hour rights of exclusive possession, one provisional bonus point per occupation, fifty-point penalty for using another class&#x27;s spot.<span class=\"pg\">p.39<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Hoshinomiya Chie<\/b> Brushes off the eavesdropping complaint and the &quot;fate&quot; line; threatens a behavioural report.<span class=\"pg\">p.38<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Ike Kanji<\/b> Refuses the emergency-bear question on process grounds: &quot;the test has already begun. I cannot answer any questions that might possibly influence your results.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.30<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Post<\/span> Confirms the school &quot;isn’t involved in the test process at all&quot; — survival is the test.<span class=\"pg\">p.33<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p10-1\">← Before: Ch.1: cold open in her POV, the Icarus gamble<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p97-1\">→ After: Ch.2 §2.8: she announces the retirement<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p97-1","v":"y1v3","y":1,"o":3,"k":"","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 2  2 8  she announces the retirement reads the class the cost of kouenji s exit without inflection   kouenji complained about being in poor physical condition and returned to the ship  of course  that means that you ve had thirty points deducted  those are the rules  so there s nothing to be done","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p97-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.2 §2.8: she announces the retirement<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V3 · p.97–98<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>1 more note<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> Reads the class the cost of Kouenji&#x27;s exit without inflection: &quot;Kouenji complained about being in poor physical condition and returned to the ship. Of course, that means that you’ve had thirty points deducted. Those are the rules, so there’s nothing to be done.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.97–98<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p24-1\">← Before: Ch.2 §2.1: she runs the rules that sink the summer<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p152-1\">→ After: Ch.5 §5.2: the faculty-room deal<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p152-1","v":"y1v3","y":1,"o":3,"k":"action spoken","w":"ayanokouji-kiyotaka chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 5  5 2  the faculty room deal closing ceremonies  she catches him the moment homeroom ends   faculty room  before he leaves   and gives nothing else away until the door shuts   ayanokouji  i have to talk to you before you leave  come to the faculty room  waits in the faculty room doorway and answers in short sentences that raise his  incoming depression  meter   come in   we ll talk inside   you might expect bad things when you re asked to come to the faculty room  but contrary to your expectations  it s a fine place  there aren t eyes all around in here  many things are better said with some personal privacy  uses a faculty room whose security camera is missing   privacy is the point of the venue   that s funny  i thought you didn t have any friends   i ve called you here today because i wanted to tell you my personal story   it s something i haven t talked to anyone about since i had become a homeroom teacher  it s silly  but please listen   don t tell this story to anyone else  if you can do that  please return to your seat   how do i appear to you as class d s homeroom teacher   am i wrong   no  it s as you say  i won t deny it  however  the truth is different  owns the cold teacher verdict outright  then opens with the reason behind it   i was once a student at this school  i was in class d  just like you  she sat where he sits   a student of this school  in class d  once   huh  well  in my time  the class difference wasn t as extreme  you could say that we were in a four way battle  not a three way one  up until we approached graduation in the third semester of our third year  the difference between a and d wasn t even 100 points  it was a close battle  where even one trivial mistake could knock you off balance  her era at the school  four classes fighting as four  a and d under 100 points apart until the third semester of the third year   set against the three way school he knows   yes  it happened rather unexpectedly  class c went to hell because of my mistake  in the end  my goal of reaching class a and my dreams were shattered  one trivial mistake sent class c down and ended her class a dream   the regret the narration reads as still live   i feel that your presence will be vital to reach class a   a few days ago  a certain man contacted the school directly  he said  expel ayanokouji kiyotaka   of course  we can t just expel someone on a third party s whim  as long as you are a student at this school  you are protected by the rules  however if you cause any problems  that s a different story  smoking  bullying  stealing  cheating  if you cause any scandal  expulsion is unavoidable   it has nothing to do with your intentions  if i determine that something seems like an issue  it will become reality  the rule she wields  protection stands until she decides something is a problem   then the scandal  not the intent  decides the expulsion   here s the deal  ayanokouji  you are going to aim for class a for me  i will follow up as extensively as i can in order to protect you  don t you think that sounds like a good offer  names it a deal and dresses the pressure as protection   the narration calls it blackmailing a student outright   that s too bad  ayanokouji  you will be expelled  and once again  class d won t reach class a   let me ask you once more  will you aim for class a  or be expelled  you choose  takes the collar grab over the desk  then laughs in self deprecation   the confidence breaks and the old dream shows   i surprised myself  i realize now that i still haven t given up on reaching class a   i d thought you would lead class d voluntarily  but we don t have any time to waste  you have to decide here and now  will you help me or not   relax  my life is already full of regrets  by the end the cold indifference is gone   misty eyed and forceful  the act put away","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p152-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.5 §5.2: the faculty-room deal<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V3 · p.152–157<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Closing ceremonies: she catches him the moment homeroom ends — faculty room, before he leaves — and gives nothing else away until the door shuts.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"ayanokouji-kiyotaka.html\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/a><\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“Ayanokouji. I have to talk to you before you leave. Come to the faculty room.”<span class=\"pg\">p.152<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“Come in.”<span class=\"pg\">p.153<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“We’ll talk inside.”<span class=\"pg\">p.153<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>31 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">What happens<\/span> Waits in the faculty-room doorway and answers in short sentences that raise his “incoming depression” meter.<span class=\"pg\">p.153<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “You might expect bad things when you’re asked to come to the faculty room, but contrary to your expectations, it’s a fine place. There aren’t eyes all around in here. Many things are better said with some personal privacy.”<span class=\"pg\">p.153<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Post<\/span> Uses a faculty room whose security camera is missing — privacy is the point of the venue.<span class=\"pg\">p.154<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “That’s funny. I thought you didn’t have any friends?”<span class=\"pg\">p.154<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I’ve called you here today because I wanted to tell you my personal story.”<span class=\"pg\">p.154<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “It’s something I haven’t talked to anyone about since I had become a homeroom teacher. It’s silly, but please listen.”<span class=\"pg\">p.154<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Don’t tell this story to anyone else. If you can do that, please return to your seat.”<span class=\"pg\">p.154<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “How do I appear to you as Class D’s homeroom teacher?”<span class=\"pg\">p.154<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Am I wrong?”<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “No, it’s as you say. I won’t deny it. However, the truth is different.”<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Owns the cold-teacher verdict outright, then opens with the reason behind it.<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I was once a student at this school. I was in Class D, just like you.”<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Fact<\/span> She sat where he sits — a student of this school, in Class D, once.<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Huh… Well, in my time, the class difference wasn’t as extreme. You could say that we were in a four-way battle, not a three-way one. Up until we approached graduation in the third semester of our third year, the difference between A and D wasn’t even 100 points. It was a close battle, where even one trivial mistake could knock you off balance.”<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> Her era at the school: four classes fighting as four, A and D under 100 points apart until the third semester of the third year — set against the three-way school he knows.<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Yes. It happened rather unexpectedly. Class C went to hell because of my mistake. In the end, my goal of reaching Class A and my dreams were shattered.”<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Limit<\/span> One trivial mistake sent Class C down and ended her Class A dream — the regret the narration reads as still live.<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I feel that your presence will be vital to reach Class A.”<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “A few days ago, a certain man contacted the school directly. He said ‘expel Ayanokouji Kiyotaka.’”<span class=\"pg\">p.155<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Of course. We can’t just expel someone on a third party’s whim. As long as you are a student at this school, you are protected by the rules. However…if you cause any problems, that’s a different story. Smoking, bullying, stealing, cheating… If you cause any scandal, expulsion is unavoidable.”<span class=\"pg\">p.156<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “It has nothing to do with your intentions. If I determine that something seems like an issue, it will become reality.”<span class=\"pg\">p.156<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> The rule she wields: protection stands until she decides something is a problem — then the scandal, not the intent, decides the expulsion.<span class=\"pg\">p.156<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Here’s the deal, Ayanokouji. You are going to aim for Class A for me. I will follow up as extensively as I can in order to protect you. Don’t you think that sounds like a good offer?”<span class=\"pg\">p.156<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Tactic<\/span> Names it a deal and dresses the pressure as protection — the narration calls it blackmailing a student outright.<span class=\"pg\">p.156<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “That’s too bad, Ayanokouji. You will be expelled, and once again, Class D won’t reach Class A.”<span class=\"pg\">p.156<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Let me ask you once more. Will you aim for Class A? Or be expelled? You choose.”<span class=\"pg\">p.156<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/b> Takes the collar grab over the desk, then laughs in self-deprecation — the confidence breaks and the old dream shows.<span class=\"pg\">p.156<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I surprised myself. I realize now that I still haven’t given up on reaching Class A.”<span class=\"pg\">p.156<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I’d thought you would lead Class D voluntarily, but we don’t have any time to waste. You have to decide here and now. Will you help me or not?”<span class=\"pg\">p.157<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Relax. My life is already full of regrets.”<span class=\"pg\">p.157<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> By the end the cold indifference is gone — misty-eyed and forceful, the act put away.<span class=\"pg\">p.157<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p97-1\">← Before: Ch.2 §2.8: she announces the retirement<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p248-1\">→ After: Ch.7: the Icarus talk<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p248-1","v":"y1v3","y":1,"o":3,"k":"spoken","w":"ayanokouji-kiyotaka chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 7  the icarus talk intercepts him on the empty side of the ship and confirms  through the icarus myth  that the man who demanded his expulsion is his father   may i have a word with you   i don t really feel sociable  is it okay if i refuse  chabashira sensei   if you really don t want to  i can just start talking here  you don t mind if we stand outside  do you   it s hot  so please keep it brief   is it safe to say that for the time being  you re satisfied   yes  first  i d like to say that you did wonderfully  i m honestly impressed   well  tell me something  is it really true  did he demand i be expelled from school   do you have any basis for saying that the story is true   i know quite a lot about you  might not that be the reason  more than anything else  other teachers don t know about your true abilities  but i have no doubts  claims knowledge of his true abilities that the other teachers do not have   i m sure that you ve heard of this rather famous myth  the wings of icarus   why do you bring that up   icarus flew out of the tower where he was imprisoned in order to gain his freedom  however  that wasn t achieved through one person s abilities alone  it was because his father  daedalus  had constructed wings and instructed him to fly  he didn t fly because of his own intentions  don t you think that sounds exactly like your current predicament   i can t understand   that man no  your father said this   sooner or later  kiyotaka will gladly chase the means of his expulsion from school  you re welcoming your end  much like how icarus fell into the sea and died after his wings were burned  because he d flown too close to the sun  the man who demanded his expulsion from the school is named as his father  with the father s own words quoted   so  what do you plan to do  she asked   you should know  sensei  icarus won t heed daedalus  warnings  answers her myth with the myth   burned wings and all  he flies on for his freedom","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p248-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.7: the Icarus talk<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V3 · p.248–249<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Intercepts him on the empty side of the ship and confirms, through the Icarus myth, that the man who demanded his expulsion is his father.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"ayanokouji-kiyotaka.html\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/a><\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“May I have a word with you?”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\"><b class=\"sp\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/b> “I don’t really feel sociable. Is it okay if I refuse, Chabashira-sensei?”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“If you really don’t want to, I can just start talking here. You don’t mind if we stand outside, do you?”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>16 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “It’s hot, so please keep it brief.”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Is it safe to say that for the time being, you’re satisfied?”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Yes. First, I’d like to say that you did wonderfully. I’m honestly impressed.”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Well, tell me something. Is it really true? Did he demand I be expelled from school?”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Do you have any basis for saying that the story is true?”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I know quite a lot about you. Might not that be the reason, more than anything else? Other teachers don’t know about your true abilities. But I have no doubts.”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Feat<\/span> Claims knowledge of his true abilities that the other teachers do not have.<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I’m sure that you’ve heard of this rather famous myth, the wings of Icarus.”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Why do you bring that up?”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Icarus flew out of the tower where he was imprisoned in order to gain his freedom. However, that wasn’t achieved through one person’s abilities alone. It was because his father, Daedalus, had constructed wings and instructed him to fly. He didn’t fly because of his own intentions. Don’t you think that sounds exactly like your current predicament?”<span class=\"pg\">p.248<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I can’t understand.”<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “That man—no, your father—said this: ‘Sooner or later, Kiyotaka will gladly chase the means of his expulsion from school.’ You’re welcoming your end, much like how Icarus fell into the sea and died after his wings were burned, because he’d flown too close to the sun.”<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Fact<\/span> The man who demanded his expulsion from the school is named as his father, with the father’s own words quoted.<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “So, what do you plan to do?” she asked.<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “You should know, Sensei. Icarus won’t heed Daedalus’ warnings.”<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/b> Answers her myth with the myth — burned wings and all, he flies on for his freedom.<span class=\"pg\">p.249<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v3-p152-1\">← Before: Ch.5 §5.2: the faculty-room deal<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4-p12-1","v":"y1v4","y":1,"o":4,"k":"","w":"ayanokouji-kiyotaka chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 2  the class a leverage  named twice named in ayanokoji s cabin reverie   the leverage that brought him to class a s track  the threat that binds him  she  threatened to expel me from this paradise  unless he works toward class a  the narrator contrasts her with mashima  where she seems  unmotivated and unnaturally uncooperative  he is  impossible to read  still holds him with the class a ultimatum three days after the island test","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4-p12-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.2: the Class A leverage, named twice<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4 · p.12, p.34<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Named in Ayanokōji’s cabin reverie — the leverage that brought him to Class A’s track.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"ayanokouji-kiyotaka.html\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/a><\/p><details class=\"more\"><summary>3 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Backstory<\/span> The threat that binds him: she “threatened to expel me from this paradise” unless he works toward Class A.<span class=\"pg\">p.12<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The narrator contrasts her with Mashima: where she seems “unmotivated and unnaturally uncooperative,” he is “impossible to read.”<span class=\"pg\">p.34<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/b> Still holds him with the Class A ultimatum three days after the island test.<span class=\"pg\">p.12<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4-p121-1\">→ After: Ch.3: in the teachers’ corner<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4-p121-1","v":"y1v4","y":1,"o":4,"k":"","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 3  in the teachers  corner she shares a sofa with hoshinomiya and mashima as ayanokoji approaches  named in the three teacher group he finds relaxing off the deck  sips her drink like a cocktail while hoshinomiya downs whiskey in one gulp  the conversation turns familiar   she is called sae chan and asked about her focus on ayanokoji","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4-p121-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.3: in the teachers’ corner<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4 · p.121–122<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">She shares a sofa with Hoshinomiya and Mashima as Ayanokōji approaches.<\/p><details class=\"more\"><summary>3 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> Named in the three-teacher group he finds relaxing off the deck.<span class=\"pg\">p.121<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> Sips her drink like a cocktail while Hoshinomiya downs whiskey in one gulp.<span class=\"pg\">p.122<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The conversation turns familiar — she is called Sae-chan and asked about her focus on Ayanokōji.<span class=\"pg\">p.122<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4-p12-1\">← Before: Ch.2: the Class A leverage, named twice<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4-p166-1\">→ After: Ch.4 §4.3: the midnight point question<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4-p166-1","v":"y1v4","y":1,"o":4,"k":"spoken","w":"hoshinomiya-chie ayanokouji-kiyotaka chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 4  4 3  the midnight point question meets ayanokoji by the night pool after his call  answers the buy anything exceptions question  survives hoshinomiya s drunk interruption  and gets asked for a future favour   sorry to keep you waiting  ayanokouji   it s okay  i m sorry for calling you so late   his line  in exchange   an instructor is obligated to consult with a student  it s nothing out of the ordinary  besides  for better or worse  this is the first time you ve actually called me  doesn t treat class d with love  students wouldn t normally come to her   his read of why the first call matters   don t worry about it  adult issues  anyway  what did you want to ask  pale  alcohol on her breath    adult issues  waved off while he works the exceptions question   yes  exceptions exist  naturally  for example  if you request to buy the life of a teacher or student  we cannot honor that request  private points cannot buy a life   the standing exception to  anything   you re drunk  aren t you  chabashira sensei said   to hoshinomiya   jeez  you can really hold your liquor  you handled yourself today  and you re fine now  too   about hoshinomiya s iron liver  grabs her by the nape and drags her off  stays calm under the sneaking around bait   ayanokouji requested this location  he wanted to consult me in private   a big problem  as a teacher  it s only natural for me to consult my students and soothe their anxieties  answered chabashira sensei   good  now hurry up and leave already  i ll head back soon myself   sorry  she s troublesome   well  i can only speak from my own experience  but it was when someone wanted   to change the school rules  of course  you re limited to a certain realistic scope  for example  you could change the class start time in order to avoid being late to class  she answered  the largest point spend she has seen  a system change within a realistic scope   hypotheticals only  no facts   are you dissatisfied with my answer   you could have just emailed me that question  i don t understand why you wanted me to meet you  accepts the no email reason  no record  eyes him with suspicion when he calls the future favour over his shoulder   i ll ask you for a favor in the near future  i called   his line  her reaction recorded  the teacher rivalry read  competing and deceiving for the top class   salaries or a school days grudge  he can t tell","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4-p166-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.4 §4.3: the midnight point question<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4 · p.166–169<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Meets Ayanokōji by the night pool after his call; answers the buy-anything exceptions question, survives Hoshinomiya’s drunk interruption, and gets asked for a future favour.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"hoshinomiya-chie.html\">Hoshinomiya Chie<\/a> · <a href=\"ayanokouji-kiyotaka.html\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/a><\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“Sorry to keep you waiting, Ayanokouji.”<span class=\"pg\">p.166<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“It’s okay. I’m sorry for calling you so late.” (his line, in exchange)<span class=\"pg\">p.166<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“An instructor is obligated to consult with a student. It’s nothing out of the ordinary. Besides, for better or worse, this is the first time you’ve actually called me.”<span class=\"pg\">p.166<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>18 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> Doesn’t treat Class D with love; students wouldn’t normally come to her — his read of why the first call matters.<span class=\"pg\">p.166<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Don’t worry about it. Adult issues. Anyway, what did you want to ask?”<span class=\"pg\">p.166<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> Pale, alcohol on her breath — “adult issues” waved off while he works the exceptions question.<span class=\"pg\">p.166<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Yes. Exceptions exist, naturally. For example, if you request to buy the life of a teacher or student, we cannot honor that request.”<span class=\"pg\">p.166<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> Private points cannot buy a life — the standing exception to “anything.”<span class=\"pg\">p.166<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “You’re drunk, aren’t you?” Chabashira-sensei said. (to Hoshinomiya)<span class=\"pg\">p.167<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Jeez. You can really hold your liquor. You handled yourself today, and you’re fine now, too.” (about Hoshinomiya’s iron liver)<span class=\"pg\">p.167<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Hoshinomiya Chie<\/b> Grabs her by the nape and drags her off; stays calm under the sneaking-around bait; “Ayanokouji requested this location. He wanted to consult me in private.”<span class=\"pg\">p.167-168<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “A big problem? As a teacher, it’s only natural for me to consult my students and soothe their anxieties,” answered Chabashira-sensei.<span class=\"pg\">p.168<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Good. Now hurry up and leave already. I’ll head back soon myself.”<span class=\"pg\">p.168<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Sorry. She’s troublesome.”<span class=\"pg\">p.168<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Well, I can only speak from my own experience, but it was when someone wanted … to change the school rules. Of course, you’re limited to a certain realistic scope. For example, you could change the class start time in order to avoid being late to class,” she answered.<span class=\"pg\">p.168-169<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Rule<\/span> The largest point spend she has seen: a system change within a realistic scope — hypotheticals only, no facts.<span class=\"pg\">p.169<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Are you dissatisfied with my answer?”<span class=\"pg\">p.169<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “You could have just emailed me that question. I don’t understand why you wanted me to meet you.”<span class=\"pg\">p.169<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/b> Accepts the no-email reason (no record); eyes him with suspicion when he calls the future favour over his shoulder.<span class=\"pg\">p.169<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “I’ll ask you for a favor in the near future,” I called. (his line, her reaction recorded)<span class=\"pg\">p.169<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Trait<\/span> The teacher rivalry read: competing and deceiving for the top class — salaries or a school-days grudge, he can’t tell.<span class=\"pg\">p.168<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4-p121-1\">← Before: Ch.3: in the teachers’ corner<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4_5-p71-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"ayanokouji-kiyotaka chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 3  a passing mention ak compares manabu s interest in him to chabashira s   the 50  entrance scores and the resume versus report gap  she is the other adult who has fixated on him for the same reason  no scene with her in this chapter","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4_5-p71-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.3: a passing mention<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.71<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">AK compares Manabu&#x27;s interest in him to Chabashira&#x27;s — the 50% entrance scores and the resume-versus-report gap.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"ayanokouji-kiyotaka.html\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/a><\/p><details class=\"more\"><summary>1 more note<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/b> She is the other adult who has fixated on him for the same reason; no scene with her in this chapter.<span class=\"pg\">p.71<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4_5-p87-1\">→ After: Ch.3: a passing mention<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4_5-p87-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"ayanokouji-kiyotaka chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 3  a passing mention ak credits his island and cruise ship restraint to the partial displays he gave her  she is the reason he had to show off on the island  horikita has no clue about the arrangement","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4_5-p87-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.3: a passing mention<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.87<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">AK credits his island and cruise-ship restraint to the partial displays he gave her.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"ayanokouji-kiyotaka.html\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/a><\/p><details class=\"more\"><summary>1 more note<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/b> She is the reason he had to show off on the island; Horikita has no clue about the arrangement.<span class=\"pg\">p.87<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4_5-p71-1\">← Before: Ch.3: a passing mention<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4_5-p159-1\">→ After: Ch.6: a passing mention<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4_5-p159-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"karuizawa-kei chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 6  a passing mention ak names her as one of the people he would bring in to eliminate karuizawa s enemies  the promise makes her the class s last resort protection behind hirata","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4_5-p159-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.6: a passing mention<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.159<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">AK names her as one of the people he would bring in to eliminate Karuizawa&#x27;s enemies.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"karuizawa-kei.html\">Karuizawa Kei<\/a><\/p><details class=\"more\"><summary>1 more note<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Karuizawa Kei<\/b> The promise makes her the class&#x27;s last-resort protection behind Hirata.<span class=\"pg\">p.159<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v4_5-p87-1\">← Before: Ch.3: a passing mention<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v5-p16-1","v":"y1v5","y":1,"o":6,"k":"spoken","w":"chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 2  2 1  the sports festival briefing afternoon homeroom runs two hours  she distributes the festival handout and weekly schedules in a detached  matter of fact manner   however  from september to the beginning of october  we will be holding more physical education classes to prepare for the school sports festival   in any case  this event will massively impact each and every class    answers hirata s special exam question vaguely  explains the red white split   class d and class a ride the red team together   you in class d are assigned to the red team   their reward will be not having any points deducted    the cruel  brief answer to the winning team question   in the sports festival  you can earn test points as a prize  which you can then apply to a written exam    lets out a slight laugh  an unusual sight  reads out the ten lowest penalty  for first years  ten points off the next written exam   should you fall short of the minimum required number of people for competitions for all participants  you will be deemed unable to continue and disqualified   a substitute requires 100 000 private points per competition   next period s location will move to gymnasium 1  where you ll meet with students from other classes and grade levels","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v5-p16-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.2 §2.1: the sports festival briefing<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V5 · p.16–25<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Afternoon homeroom runs two hours: she distributes the festival handout and weekly schedules in a detached, matter-of-fact manner.<\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“However, from September to the beginning of October, we will be holding more physical education classes to prepare for the school sports festival.”<span class=\"pg\">p.16<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“In any case, this event will massively impact each and every class.” — answers Hirata’s special-exam question vaguely.<span class=\"pg\">p.16<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“You in Class D are assigned to the Red Team.”<span class=\"pg\">p.17<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>7 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Post<\/span> Explains the Red/White split — Class D and Class A ride the Red Team together.<span class=\"pg\">p.17<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Their reward will be not having any points deducted.” — the cruel, brief answer to the winning-team question.<span class=\"pg\">p.18<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “In the sports festival, you can earn test points as a prize, which you can then apply to a written exam.” — lets out a slight laugh, an unusual sight.<span class=\"pg\">p.20<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Post<\/span> Reads out the ten-lowest penalty: for first-years, ten points off the next written exam.<span class=\"pg\">p.20<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Should you fall short of the minimum required number of people for competitions for all participants, you will be deemed unable to continue and disqualified,”<span class=\"pg\">p.22<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “A substitute requires 100,000 private points per competition.”<span class=\"pg\">p.23<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “Next period’s location will move to Gymnasium 1, where you’ll meet with students from other classes and grade levels.”<span class=\"pg\">p.23<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v5-p35-1\">→ After: Ch.2 §2.3: the gate<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v5-p35-1","v":"y1v5","y":1,"o":6,"k":"spoken","w":"ayanokouji-kiyotaka chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 2  2 3  the gate spraying water at the main gate after school  she s on duty and clocks that he stayed behind later than usual   you stayed behind longer than usual  ayanokouji   are you prepared for the sports festival   in your case  i would have thought that you d already have some eccentric idea or strategy in place   but if you don t show me some results soon  i ll stop covering for you  their talk stops when their eyes meet   public discussion helps no one  she accepts his right to choose his path but puts a deadline on her cover  blank short scan page sits inside the dialogue  footer and watermark only","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v5-p35-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.2 §2.3: the gate<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V5 · p.35–37<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Spraying water at the main gate after school; she’s on duty and clocks that he stayed behind later than usual.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"ayanokouji-kiyotaka.html\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/a><\/p><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">“You stayed behind longer than usual, Ayanokouji.”<span class=\"pg\">p.35<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“Are you prepared for the sports festival?”<span class=\"pg\">p.37<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q spoken\">“In your case, I would have thought that you’d already have some eccentric idea or strategy in place,”<span class=\"pg\">p.37<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>3 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Spoken<\/span> “But if you don’t show me some results soon, I’ll stop covering for you.”<span class=\"pg\">p.37<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Ayanokōji Kiyotaka<\/b> Their talk stops when their eyes meet — public discussion helps no one; she accepts his right to choose his path but puts a deadline on her cover.<span class=\"pg\">p.37<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> Blank/short scan page sits inside the dialogue (footer and watermark only).<span class=\"pg\">p.36<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v5-p16-1\">← Before: Ch.2 §2.1: the sports festival briefing<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v5-p39-1\">→ After: Ch.3 §3: Watches the homeroom from the back<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v5-p39-1","v":"y1v5","y":1,"o":6,"k":"","w":"hirata-yousuke chabashira-sae","q":"chabashira sae ch 3  3  watches the homeroom from the back chabashira moves to the back of the classroom during hirata s two hour homeroom and does not speak   she intends to watch the class run itself  lets hirata run the session unopposed   she speaks no word during the whole strategy meeting  the chapter prints nothing further about her  her last stated posture is watching from the back  p 39","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v5-p39-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.3 §3: Watches the homeroom from the back<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V5 · p.39–53<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Chabashira moves to the back of the classroom during Hirata’s two-hour homeroom and does not speak — she intends to watch the class run itself.<\/p><p class=\"with\">with <a href=\"hirata-yousuke.html\">Hirata Yōsuke<\/a><\/p><details class=\"more\"><summary>2 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Relationship<\/span> <b class=\"sp\">Hirata Yōsuke<\/b> Lets Hirata run the session unopposed — she speaks no word during the whole strategy meeting.<span class=\"pg\">p.39<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The chapter prints nothing further about her; her last stated posture is watching from the back (p.39).<span class=\"pg\">p.41<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-chabashira-sae-y1v5-p35-1\">← Before: Ch.2 §2.3: the gate<\/button><\/div><\/article>"}];
